  "response_text": "Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it can cause starvation of lower-priority threads. If a high-priority thread is always ready to run (e.g., due to constant interrupts or yielding), the lower-priority threads may never get CPU time. This breaks fairness and can cause important background tasks (like I/O handling or cleanup) to be completely ignored. Round-robin or priority-based scheduling with aging or time slices can prevent this issue by giving all tasks a chance to run.\\n",
